	abstract = {he island of Crete is well known for its richness in endemic plant and animal species and subspecies. The study of the differentiation of these taxa using clustering methods has shown that endemic plants form two distinct groups, one in the high mountain areas and one in the lowlands. On the contrary, animals form three distinct groups, one in the western, one in the centre and one in the eastern part of the island. This reflects the different strategies of colonization and of survival to adverse conditions such as the break-up of the island during the Pleiocene and the invasion of humans.},
